Yes — reproduced deterministically, and the reporter's diagnosis is exactly right.

## The repro

I added `apps/backend/tests/mcp-runtime-race.test.ts`, which stands up a temp project with three stdio servers in `agent/mcps/mcp.json` and no spec dirs (the startup path from the issue), and mocks `mcporter` with a fake runtime that mirrors the real `McpRuntime` semantics: its own `definitions` map per instance, and `Unknown MCP server '<name>'` thrown from `listTools`/`callTool` for anything not in it.

Against unpatched `main` it fails with the issue's exact log signature:

```
[ERROR] [tool] MCP discovery failed: alpha
[ERROR] [tool] MCP discovery failed: beta
[ERROR] [tool] MCP tool call failed: alpha/alpha_tool
[ERROR] [tool] MCP tool call failed: beta/beta_tool

AssertionError: expected [ FakeRuntime{...}, ...(2) ] to have a length of 1 but got 3
AssertionError: [{ reason: Error { message: "Unknown MCP server 'alpha'." }, status: 'rejected' },
                 { reason: Error { message: "Unknown MCP server 'beta'." },  status: 'rejected' }]
```

Three runtimes for three servers, and only the last one to resolve survives. The ordering is fully deterministic in the microtask queue, which is why it looks random in production but is reliable in a test: all three `_ensureRegistered` calls clear the `!this._runtime` guard before any `createRuntime()` resolves, then each resumes in turn, assigns its own runtime, and registers its server on it. By the time `alpha`'s `listTools` actually runs, `this._runtime` is `gamma`'s instance. `gamma` always wins; `alpha` and `beta` always lose. In the real deployment which server wins varies only because `createRuntime()` does file I/O whose completion order isn't fixed.

Worth noting the second failure mode the reporter mentions is also confirmed: after the botched discovery, `_registered` contains all three names, so subsequent `callTool`s skip re-registration entirely and hit the surviving runtime, which has never seen those definitions. That's the "tool calls fail in ~30 ms" part — no connection attempt, just a map miss.

## The fix

I applied the memoized-promise fix from the issue in `apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts` and both tests pass with the error logs gone. Existing `mcp-review-fixes.test.ts` (13 tests) still passes, and `npm run lint -w @nao/backend` is clean.

```747:761:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
	/**
	 * Memoizes the in-flight `createRuntime()` so parallel discoveries share one runtime.
	 * Without it each concurrent caller builds its own and overwrites `_runtime`, stranding
	 * the definitions registered on the discarded instances.
	 */
	private _sharedRuntime(): Promise<Runtime> {
		if (!this._runtimePromise) {
			this._runtimePromise = createRuntime().catch((error) => {
				this._runtimePromise = null;
				throw error;
			});
		}
		return this._runtimePromise;
	}
```

One addition beyond the reporter's patch: `_resetRuntime()` also has to null `_runtimePromise`, otherwise a config reload would keep handing back the pre-reload runtime and definitions for deleted servers would linger.

Since @beherap offered to send a PR, you may want to keep the fix for them and land just the test — happy to split it that way.

I've completed the work and should just report the findings. There are unrelated file modifications already in the working tree that I shouldn't commit, so I'll present the microtask trace and results without making any changes.

