Yes, it's a smell, and worth not merging as-is. I refactored to remove the need for it and verified the result.

## Why the loop is there

It isn't guarding against anything intrinsic — it's compensating for state that can disagree with itself. `McpService` tracks the runtime across three places: `_runtime`, `_registered`, and the in-flight creation promise. A reload can advance one without the others, so the loop exists to make the losers of a race notice they lost and re-converge on the winner. Two concrete costs: it's an unbounded retry with no cap, and it obscures the actual defect, which is that `_callToolMcporter` and `_listTools` both do `await this._ensureRegistered(...)` and *then* re-read `this._runtime`. That re-read after an await is where the stale instance leaks in. The loop makes the window smaller instead of closing it.

## What I did instead

Pair the runtime with the set of servers registered on it, and have `_ensureRegistered` return the runtime it registered on:

```739:772:apps/backend/src/services/mcp.ts
	private async _ensureRegistered(name: string): Promise<Runtime> {
		const config = this._mcpServers[name];
		if (!config) {
			throw new Error(`MCP server "${name}" is not configured.`);
		}
		const session = await this._session();
		if (!session.registered.has(name)) {
			session.runtime.registerDefinition(this._toServerDefinition(name, config), { overwrite: true });
			session.registered.add(name);
		}
		return session.runtime;
	}
```

Callers now use the returned value, so a caller can never act on a runtime other than the one it registered against. `_runtime` and `_registered` are gone as separate fields, `_resetRuntime()` is a single assignment, and the two dead `throw new Error('MCP runtime not initialized')` branches disappear because the runtime is no longer nullable at the point of use. No loop, no identity check on publish. The one identity check that remains is in the failure path, clearing the memo only if it's still the current one.

## Verification

I didn't want to trust reasoning again after being wrong about the reload case, so I checked all three variants against the suite. I also rewrote the third test to assert through the public `callTool` rather than poking at internal fields, and to release the gated creations newest-first, which is the order that actually exposes a stale publish.

| | parallel startup | call after discovery | reload mid-creation |
|---|---|---|---|
| `main` | fail | fail | fail |
| naive memoization | pass | pass | fail |
| session pairing | pass | pass | pass |

The middle row matters: it proves the third test is load-bearing rather than passing by construction. `npm run lint -w @nao/backend` is clean and the existing 13 MCP tests still pass.

## One honest tradeoff

There is a behavioural difference from beherap's version. With the loop, a discovery whose creation was superseded gets pulled onto the new runtime. With sessions, it completes against its own. I think completing coherently is the better semantic — that discovery was started against the pre-reload config — but it does mean the old runtime may have connected clients when it's dropped. That feeds the pre-existing leak I mentioned: `_resetRuntime()` never calls `runtime.close()`, so orphaned stdio child processes survive every reload. Sessions slightly widen that window in the rare reload-mid-creation case. It's the same leak either way, not a new one, but fixing it properly is the natural follow-up and would make this choice moot.

The while loop is a code smell because it's trying to reconcile three pieces of state that can drift across different generations—the runtime, registered set, and in-flight promise. If I pair the runtime with its registered set and return both together to callers, there's nothing left to converge on, eliminating the need for the loop entirely.

This approach also removes two dead code branches that checked for uninitialized runtimes. The tradeoff is real though: in-flight discovery from the old generation now completes against its own runtime rather than being forced onto the new one, which means in the rare case of a reload mid-creation, the old runtime's connections get orphaned without a proper close. That said, the whole old runtime is already dropped unclosed on reload anyway, so this doesn't introduce a new kind of leak—just slightly widens the window in that edge case. I should be honest about that rather than pretend the refactor is purely better.

